* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈpɛnd/|/di-/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-depend.ogg|a=US}}
** {{IPA|en|/dəˈpɛnd/|a=weak vowel}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈpend/|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/dəˈpend/|a=NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛnd|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[contingent]] or [[conditioned]]; to have something as a necessary [[condition]] {{+obj|en|:on/:upon|:of<l:obsolete>}}
#: {{syn|en|hinge on|ride|pivot}}
#: {{ux|en|We would like to go skiing, but it '''depends''' on the amount of snow.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|actor=Humphrey Bogart|role=Dobbs|title=The Treasure of the Sierra Madre|writer=John Huston|publisher=|year=1948|passage=Gold don't carry any curse with it. It all '''depends''' on whether or not the guy who finds it is the right guy. The way I see it, gold can be as much of a blessing as a curse}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:National Geographic|year=1990|month=August|volume=178|number=2|title=Changing Images of the Northwest Passage|author=John Bockstoce|page=18
|text=There's not much room for error in the unforgiving Arctic wilderness, where hunters' lives '''depend''' on skill, experience, and a buddy's helping hand.}}
# {{senseid|en|trust}}{{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[trust]]; to have confidence; to [[rely]]. {{+obj|en|:on/:upon<qq:usually>}}
#: {{ux|en|we should all be able to '''depend''' on the word or assurance of our friends}}
#: {{ux|en|we '''depend''' on the mailman to come at the usual time.}}
#* {{RQ:Nesbit New Treasure Seekers|passage="It's another wedding present, you may '''depend'''," Dicky said—"a beastly surprise, I shouldn't wonder."}}
# {{lb|en|now|literary|heraldry}} To hang down; to be sustained by being fastened or attached to something above, especially in heraldry, where a badge, decoration, or element is suspended from another part of an achievement of arms.
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|passage=The long rows of teeth on the bulwarks glistened in the moonlight; and like the white ivory tusks of some huge elephant, vast curving icicles '''depended''' from the bows.}}
#* {{RQ:Conrad Secret Agent|passage=Bronze chandeliers with many globes '''depended''' from the low, slightly vaulted ceiling, and the fresco paintings ran flat and dull all round the walls without windows, representing scenes of the chase and of outdoor revelry in medieval costumes.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Lost World|passage=He had prepared a sort of collar of leather with many straps '''depending''' from it.}}
#* {{Q|en|{{w|Thomas Flanagan (writer)|Thomas Flanagan}}|novel: The Tenants of Time|chapter 13|year=1988|quote=At the wedding feast of Agnes and Bob, all was meant for the best, and there was grewat good humour, but there was also a stiffness, a constraint.  It did not touch them: Agnes was radiant in a gown of white silksand laces which would not have disgraced the Castle itself, her fair hair bound and coiled after the fashion of that year, and wearing her father's special gift to her, ''a necklace of interwoven gold and silver from which '''depended''' a fiery stone'', intricately cut, and housed within elaborate gold, thin-hammered.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To be pending; to be undetermined or undecided.
#: {{ux|en|a cause '''depending''' in court}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1703|title=The History Of King William The Third. In III Parts
|passage=While the Bishops Affair was '''depending''', the King sent orders [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1836|title=Reports of Cases Adjudged in the Court of King's Bench
|passage=In perjury, the capias, warrant, and affidavit, are good evidence that a cause was '''depending'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1837|title=The Acts and Monuments of John Foxe|page=544
|passage="A Letter of the King sent to his Proctors at Rome, concerning a Case of his in the said Court '''depending'''."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to be contingent or dependent on; to set as a necessity.
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Age of Consent|year=1962|page=65|passage=There he wilted, obviously '''depending''' the disposal of his person and his plight on Bradly, and expecting that to be done at once, too.}}

